        context_item("Activity 1 — Activity Synthesis", "• Display the “Two Equal Groups” t-chart with “no\nleftovers” and “some leftovers” labeled as the\n\ncategories.\n\n• Invite students to share a total amount and whether\nthey made 2 equal groups with no leftovers or 2\nequal groups with some leftovers.\n\n• Record the total in the appropriate column of the\nchart.\n• Ask students to share the number in their share and\nin their partner’s share. Record by drawing a circle\naround each share. For example:\n\n• Repeat to gather 2–3 examples of even and 2–3\nexamples of odd numbers.\n• “Look at the numbers in each category. What do you\nnotice? What do you wonder?” (I notice all the\nnumbers show 2 equal groups. I notice there’s only\never 1 leftover. I notice some numbers have the\nsame equal groups, like 6 has 2 groups of 3 and 7\nhas 2 groups of 3. I wonder why there’s only ever 1\nleftover.)\nMLR8 Discussion Supports\n\n• Before students share, remind them to use words,\nsuch as “equal groups” and “leftovers.”\n• 1 minute: quiet think time\n\n• 1–2 minutes: partner discussion\n• Share responses.\n• If needed, ask, “How many leftovers did each number\nhave?”\n• “Whenever we put objects into 2 equal groups, we\nwill either have 0 leftovers or 1 leftover.”", line(201)),
