        context_item("Activity 2 — Activity Synthesis", "• Select previously identified students to share their\nwork on the last expression.\nMLR3 Critique, Correct, Clarify\n\n• Display the response for students to consider:\n\nI added the ones, the tens, and then the hundreds.\n• Read the explanation aloud.\n• “What parts of this response are unclear, incorrect,\n\nor incomplete?” (It tells the order of places they\nadded, but it's missing a lot of details. It doesn't\nexplain what all the numbers under 670 and 257\nmean. It doesn't explain why there's 100 and 800.)\n• 1 minute: quiet think time\n• 2 minute: partner discussion\n• Invite 2 or 3 groups to share what they discussed.\nRecord for all to see.\n• “With your partner, work together to write a revised\nexplanation.”\n• Display and review the following criteria:\n◦ An explanation for each step.\n◦ Words such as “first,” “next,” and “then.”\n• 3–5 minute: partner work time\n• Select 1 or 2 groups to read their revised draft aloud\nslowly enough to record for all to see. Scribe as each\ngroup shares, and then invite the whole class to\ncontribute additional language and edits to make the\nfinal draft even clearer and more convincing. (First I\nstacked the numbers vertically. Then I added the\nones to get 7, and recorded a 7 below the ones place.\nNext I added 70 and 50 and got 120. I recorded 20\nbelow the 7, and 100 below the 20. I added 600 and\n200 to get 800, and recorded that below the 100.\nThen I added and wrote 927 as the answer.)\n• “What is the same and what is different about the\nexplanations?” (Both explanations add the ones, the\ntens, and the hundreds, but the revised explanation\ngives more detail about how to record each step.)\n• “How is this way of recording this work the same as\nor different from Han’s method in the first activity?”\n(Han would record 120 in the second row where we\nrecord the tens. It was 12 tens, but that’s the same as\n1 hundred and 2 tens. We could record 100 on one\nline and 20 on the next line.)", line(280)),
