    [
        context_item("Warm-up — Student Task Statement", "Decide if each statement is true or false. Be\nprepared to explain your reasoning.\n• 4 × 10 = 40 × 1\n\n• 4 × 20 = 4 × 2 × 10\n\n• 8 × 20 = 8 × 2 × 1\n• 8 × 20 = 16 × 10", line(97)),
        context_item("Activity 1 — Student Task Statement", "1. Use base-ten blocks to represent each\nexpression. Then, find its value.\na. 55 ÷ 5\nb. 45 ÷ 3\n2. Find the value of each expression. Use base-\nten blocks if you find them helpful.\na. 63 ÷ 3\nb. 84 ÷ 7\nc. 100 ÷ 5", line(167)),
        context_item("Activity 2 — Student Task Statement", "Jada and Han used base-ten blocks to represent\n60 ÷ 5.\nHere is Jada’s work:\n\nHere’s Han’s work:\n\n1. Make sense of Jada’s and Han’s work.\n\na. What did they do differently?\n\nb. Where do you see the value of 60 ÷ 5 in\n\neach person’s work?\n\n2. How would you use base-ten blocks to\nrepresent these expressions and find their\n\nvalue? Be prepared to explain your reasoning.\n\na. 64 ÷ 4: Would you make 4 groups or\n\ngroups of 4?\n\nb. 72 ÷ 6: Would you make 6 groups or\n\ngroups of 6?\n\nc. 75 ÷ 15: Would you make 15 groups or\ngroups of 15?", line(264)),
        context_item("Cool-down — Student Task Statement", "Find the value of 51 ÷ 3. Use base-ten blocks if they are helpful. Explain or show your reasoning.", line(372))
    ],
    [
        context_item("Warm-up — Activity Synthesis", "• “How can you justify your answer without finding the\nvalue of both sides?”\n\n• Consider asking:\n\n◦ “Who can restate _____’s reasoning in a different\n\nway?”\n◦ “Does anyone want to add on to _____’s\nreasoning?”", line(110)),
        context_item("Activity 1 — Activity Synthesis", "• Invite students to share their responses and\nreasoning for the last set of quotients.\n• Ask students who used base-ten blocks or drew\ndiagrams, “Was it necessary to decompose any of the\ntens into ones to divide?” (It wasn’t necessary for\n63 ÷ 3 because there was already the right number\nof tens and ones to put into 3 groups. It wasn’t\nnecessary for 100 ÷ 5 because I started with 10 tens\nand there was already the right number of tens to\nput into 5 groups.)\n• “Why was it necessary or helpful to decompose the\ntens in 84?” (After putting 7 tens in 7 groups, there’s\nstill 1 ten and 4 ones. The 1 ten couldn’t be split into\n7 groups.)", line(218)),
        context_item("Lesson Synthesis", "“Today, we recalled that the divisor in a division expression can be seen as the number of groups or the size of each\ngroup.”\n\nDisplay: 96 ÷ 8\n\n“If you are representing this quotient with base-ten blocks, would you put 9 tens and 6 ones in 8 groups, or would you\nput them into groups of 8?” (I would put them into 8 groups. Eight of the tens can go into 8 groups easily. The 1\nremaining ten and 6 ones make 16 ones, so 2 ones go in each group. I would put them into groups of 8. I know 10\ngroups of 8 is 80, so that takes care of the 8 tens. The 1 remaining ten and 6 ones make 16, which is 2 groups of 8.)", line(337))
    ],
    source('curriculum/im_teacher_guides/grade3/unit4/lesson19.md')).

