        context_item("Warm-up — Student Task Statement", "Decide whether each statement is true or false. Be\nprepared to explain your reasoning.\n• 16 × 1/4 = 4\n8 × 3/4 = 12\n•\n\n• 32 × 2/8 = 8\n\n• 60 × 1/12 = 10", line(97)),
        context_item("Activity 2 — Student Task Statement", "Your teacher will give you either a problem card or\na data card. Do not show or read your card to your\npartner.\n\nPause here so your teacher can review your work.\n\nAsk your teacher for a new set of cards and repeat\nthe activity, trading roles with your partner.", line(269)),
        context_item("Cool-down — Student Task Statement", "Priya needs sand and rocks for her fish tank. She needs 3 pounds of sand.\nThat amount is 4 times as much as the amount of rocks that she needs.\nHow many ounces of rocks does she need? Explain or show your\nreasoning.", line(443))
    ],
    [
        context_item("Warm-up — Activity Synthesis", "• “Can you tell whether an equation is true by looking\nat the sizes of the whole numbers and the fractions,\nwithout performing computation? For instance,\nwithout multiplying 60 × 1/12, can we say that 60 × 1/12\ncan’t be 10? How?” (Yes, we know that 12 groups of\n\n1/12 make 1, which means 120, not 60, groups of 1/12\n\nare needed to make 10.)", line(110)),
        context_item("Activity 1 — Activity Synthesis", "• Invite 1–2 students to share how they solved the\nproblem.\n• “What were the important quantities to know to\nsolve this problem?”\n• “Which questions that you asked helped you find out\nthose quantities?”", line(209)),
        context_item("Activity 2 — Activity Synthesis", "• “What were the important quantities to know to\nsolve the first problem? What about in the second\nproblem?”\n• “Did anyone solve the problem in a different way\nthan their partner?”\n• “How did you compare 9 hours and 90 minutes?”\n• “How did you find out if Noah spends more than or\nless than 4 hours with his family on a weekend day?”", line(303)),
        context_item("Lesson Synthesis", "“Today we solved measurement problems in which not all of the necessary information was provided.”\n\n“How was that experience different from other problem-solving experiences you had so far?” (We had to think about\nwhat information was needed, and also about how to ask questions that would give what we needed.)\n\n“What did you find interesting? What did you find challenging?” (We had to explain why we asked for certain pieces of\ninformation, which wasn’t always easy.)", line(417))
    ],
    source('curriculum/im_teacher_guides/grade4/unit5/lesson13.md')).

