    [
        context_item("Activity 2 — Activity Synthesis", "• “What information did you use in each situation to\nknow how to draw each diagram?”\n◦ The number of repeating rectangles in the\ngreater quantity represents “times as many.”\n◦ The row with one rectangle is the amount being\nmultiplied.\n\n◦ The row with multiple rectangles represents the\ngreater quantity in the comparison.\n• “How is the last problem different from the first\ntwo?” (The unknown value is how many “times as\nmany.”)", line(341)),
        context_item("Lesson Synthesis", "“Today we used diagrams to compare two large quantities.”\n\nDisplay some diagrams students created to represent ___ × 12 = 72\n\n“What do you notice about the different diagrams we used to represent this equation?” (6 sets of 12 or 12 sections, with\n6 in each)\n\n“Why might we represent the diagram with a number, instead of drawing out all the parts?” (Drawing all the amounts\nwould take a while and may result in a counting mistake or take up too much space.)\n\n“Diagrams that use numbers to show the quantities are a helpful tool for showing ‘_____ times as many’ situations\nbecause they can represent any amount.”", line(374))
