    [
        context_item("Warm-up — Student Task Statement", "What do you notice? What do you wonder?", line(99)),
        context_item("Activity 1 — Student Task Statement", "1. Mai walked around a soccer field 2 times. She\nthinks she walked about 1 kilometer. Do you\nagree with Mai? Explain or show your\nreasoning.\n\n2. Decide whether each distance is less than 1\nkilometer, about 1 kilometer, or greater than 1\nkilometer.\na. the distance across the state you live in\nb. the distance from your home to school\nc. the distance from your school room to the\n\nrestroom\nd. the distance you travel on a vacation in the\n\ncar", line(168)),
        context_item("Activity 2 — Student Task Statement", "The Great Pacific Garbage Patch is a large area in\nthe Pacific Ocean where trash has accumulated. By\n2022, some estimates indicated that the garbage\ncovered about 1,600,000 square kilometers of the\nsurface of the ocean.\n\n1. Rhode Island is the smallest state. It is about\n77 kilometers long and 60 kilometers wide.\nWhich is larger, the garbage patch or Rhode\nIsland? Explain or show your reasoning.\n2. Delaware is about 154 kilometers long and 48\nkilometers wide. Which is larger, the garbage\npatch or Delaware? Explain or show your\nreasoning.\n\n3. New Mexico is about 596 kilometers long and\n552 kilometers wide. Which is larger, the\ngarbage patch or New Mexico?\n\n4. Circle an area on the map of the continental\nUnited States that estimates the area of the\ngarbage patch. Explain or show your\nreasoning.", line(267)),
        context_item("Cool-down — Student Task Statement", "Wyoming is 600 kilometers wide and 452 kilometers long. What is the area of Wyoming?", line(374))
    ],
    [
        context_item("Activity 1 — Activity Synthesis", "• Invite students to share their responses for how far it\n\nis from school to where they live.\n\n• “How many meters are there in a kilometer?” (1,000)\n• “About how many steps does it take to go 1 meter?”\n(2)\n• “About how many steps does it take to go 1,000\nmeters or 1 kilometer?” (2,000)\n• “Does 2,000 steps help communicate the length of 1\nkilometer?” (Yes. That's a lot, but I can count to 1,000\nand take that many steps, so it’s not too far. No.\n1,000 steps is a lot, too much for me to imagine.)", line(204)),
        context_item("Activity 2 — Activity Synthesis", "• Invite students to share their calculations for\nDelaware.\n• “Is Delaware close in size to the garbage patch? How\ndo you know?” (No, it is not close at all. 1,600,000\nsquare kilometers is a lot more than 7,392 square\nkilometers.)\n• “Is the garbage patch greater than or less than 10\ntimes as large as Delaware?” (Much greater, because\nthat’s only about 70,000 square kilometers, which is\nway less than a million.)\n\n• “Is the garbage patch greater than or less than 100\ntimes as large as Delaware?” (Greater, because that’s\nabout 700,000 square kilometers, and the garbage\npatch is more than double that.)\n• “How could you estimate the area of New\nMexico?”(596 is close to 600, which is a nice number.\n552 is harder, but if I say that’s about 500, then I get\nan estimate of 600 × 500 or 300,000.)\n• “How did you decide which area to circle on the\n\nmap?”(5 New Mexicos would make about 1,600,000\n\nsquare kilometers, so I circled New Mexico and 4\n\nother states close in size to it.)", line(303)),
        context_item("Lesson Synthesis", "“Today we looked at the Great Pacific Garbage Patch which has an area of about 1,600,000 square kilometers. We saw\nhow big that is by comparing it to different states.”\n\n“If the Great Pacific Garbage Patch was a rectangle, what could be its side lengths?” (It could be 1,600,000 kilometers by\n1 kilometer but 1,600,000 kilometers is too long. It could be 1,250 kilometers by 1,250 kilometers. I could multiply one of\nthe lengths of New Mexico by 5, so it could be about 3,000 kilometers by 550 kilometers.)\n\n“Much of the garbage in the Great Pacific Garbage Patch is plastic. Tomorrow we will investigate the amount of\nrecyclable plastic we produce each year.”", line(341))
    ],
    source('curriculum/im_teacher_guides/grade5/unit4/lesson18.md')).

