    [
        context_item("Student Task Statement", "The table shows the relationship between Han's age and the age of a neighbor, a high school student.\n\n|   Han's age (years) |   neighbor's age (years) |\n|---------------------|--------------------------|\n|                   6 |                          |\n|                  12 |                       17 |\n|                  18 |                          |\n|                     |                       25 |\n\n\n\n1. Complete the table to show their ages.\n2. Describe the relationship between the ages of the two students in two ways.", line(113)),
        context_item("Student Task Statement", "Lin needs to mix a specific shade of orange paint for the set of the school play. The color is a mixture of red and yellow paint.\n\n1. Complete the table to show different combinations of red and yellow paint that will make the shade of orange Lin needs.\n2. Use the values in the table to create two graphs that can represent the relationship between cups of red paint and cups of yellow paint.\n\n|   cups of red paint |   cups of yellow paint |\n|---------------------|------------------------|\n|                   2 |                      3 |\n|                   6 |                        |\n|                     |                     12 |\n|                  12 |                        |\n|                     |                     21 |\n|                   1 |                        |\n|                     |                      1 |\n\n\n\n\n\n3. Describe the relationship between cups of red paint and cups of yellow paint in as many ways as you can. Pause here for a discussion.\n4. Lin writes this equation to figure out the amount of yellow paint she will need if she knows the amount of red paint being used. In this equation, represents cups of yellow paint and represents cups of red paint.\n\n$$y = \\frac { 3 } { 2 } r$$\n\nDo you agree that the equation represents the quantities in the situation? Explain your reasoning.\n\n5. What equation can we write to figure out the amount of red paint needed if we know the amount of yellow paint? Be prepared to explain how you know.", line(249)),
        context_item("Student Task Statement", "To remove grease from kitchen surfaces, a recipe says to use 1 cup of baking soda for every cup of water.\n\n5 min\n\n\n|   cups of baking soda |   cups of water |\n|-----------------------|-----------------|\n|                     1 |                 |\n|                     2 |               1 |\n|                     3 |                 |\n\n1. Which graph represents the relationship between cups of baking soda and cups of water? Explain how you know.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n$$O r$$\n\nor\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Select all equations that can represent the relationship between , cups of baking soda, and , cups of water, in this situation.\n2. A.\n3. B.\n4. C.\n5. D.\n6. E.", line(491))
    ],
    [
        context_item("Activity Synthesis", "Invite students to share their descriptions of the relationship between Han's and his neighbor's ages, and to explain how those relationships can be seen in the table.\n\nTo highlight that two equations can be written to represent the same relationship, ask students:\n\n- 'Let's say represents Han's age in years and represents his neighbor's age. What equations can we write to represent the relationship between Han's and his neighbor's ages?' ( and .)\n- 'If we know Han's age, which equation would help us find his neighbor's age?' ( )\n- 'If we know Han's neighbor's age, which equation would help us find Han's age?' ( )\n\nNext, display the following two graphs for all to see. Give students a minute to observe the graphs.", line(147)),
        context_item("Activity Synthesis", "Invite students to share whether they agree that the equation Lin wrote relates the amount of yellow paint to red paint and explain their reasoning. Then discuss the equation that students wrote for the last question.\n\nIf no students mentioned using the values in the table to check Lin's equation or to help write a second equation, ask them to discuss this idea.\n\nExplain that in a situation that has two quantities that change in relation to each other, we can use the words 'dependent' and 'independent' to describe the two variables:\n\n- If we know the amount of red paint, we can use it to calculate the amount of yellow paint, as Lin has done by writing .\n\nIn this case, we say that the amount of yellow paint depends on the amount of red paint, so is the dependent variable and is the independent variable .\n\n- If we know the amount of yellow paint, we can use it to calculate the amount of red paint, as students have done by writing .\n\nIn this case, we say that the amount of red paint depends on the amount of yellow paint so, is the dependent variable and is the independent variable\n\nDraw students' attention to the two graphs they created. Ask:\n\n- 'Which equation is represented by the first graph?' ( ) 'How can we tell?' (Multiplying the horizontal or first value of the ordered pairs by gives the second or vertical value.)", line(378)),
        context_item("Lesson Synthesis", "To summarize the major ideas in this lesson, prompt students to refer to their work in the two activities. Discuss questions such as:\n\n- 'How are the relationships between the quantities in the two situations-about ages and about paint mixture-alike?' (The value of one quantity depends on the other. The quantities can be represented using a table and graphs. We can write two equations and create two graphs for each relationship.)\n- 'How are they different?' (In the situation about ages, we added or subtracted the value of one quantity by a number to get the other. In the situation about paint mixture, we multiplied one quantity by a number to get the other.)\n- 'How do we know what equations to write to describe the relationship between the quantities?' (We can think about how to find the second quantity if we know the first quantity, and the other way around. We can use a table and think about how to complete the value in one column if we have the value in the other column.)\n\nConsider annotating the relationship between the pair of quantities in the table for each situation, as shown.\n\n\n\n\n\nor\n\n\n\nor", line(404))
    ],
    source('hermes/app/runtime/experiments/gemma4_tutor/docling/full-output/TeacherLessonGuides/Grade6/Grade6-6-16-Lesson-teacher-guide-/document.md')).

