        context_item("Activity Synthesis", "The goal of this discussion is to reinforce the exponent rule for raising powers of 10 to another power. Introduce and explain the visual display prepared earlier. This display should be kept visible to students throughout the remainder of the unit.\n\n$$t h e u n t . & & \\text {Rule} & & \\exp \\text {example showing how it works} \\\\ & & ( 1 0 ^ { n } ) ^ { m } & = 1 0 ^ { n - m } & ( 1 0 ^ { 2 } ) ^ { 3 } = ( 1 0 \\cdot 1 0 ) \\cdot ( 1 0 \\cdot 1 0 ) \\cdot ( 1 0 \\cdot 1 0 ) = 1 0 ^ { 6 } & & \\text {three groups of} & & \\frac { \\sin \\text { factors} } { \\tan \\text { factors} } & & \\text {that are ten} \\\\ & & & & & & \\text {two factors that are ten} & & \\text {that are ten} \\\\ & & & & & & & \\text {Continue to reinforce student understanding of this rule by writing out an expanded form of each expression when}$$\n\nContinue to reinforce student understanding of this rule by writing out an expanded form of each expression when discussing the following questions:\n\n- 'What is written as a single power of 10?' ( )\n- 'What is written as a single power of 10?' ( )\n- 'What are some different ways to write as a power of 10 raised to another power?' ( , , )\n\n\n\n\n\n\n4", line(229)),
        context_item("Lesson Synthesis", "The purpose of this discussion is to emphasize the similarities and differences between the rule for multiplying values with the same base and the rule for raising a value with an exponent to another power. Display these two statements for all to see, and explain that Andre and Elena were both trying to write with a single exponent.\n\n$$A n d r e \\colon 1$$\n\n$$E l e n a \\colon$$\n\nAsk students if they agree with either Andre or Elena. Give students time to share their reasoning with a partner before inviting students to share their thinking with the class. Record student responses for all to see. Consider asking the following questions for discussion:\n\n- 'How are these statements the same? How are they different?' (Both statements are attempting to write as a number with a single exponent. The first statement uses the rule for multiplying values with the same base while the second statement tries to use the rule for raising a value with an exponent to another power.)\n- 'How can the second statement be corrected to make it true?' (When raising a value with an exponent to another power, the two exponents can be multiplied together, giving .)\n- 'How can be written with exponents instead of repeated multiplication?' ( )", line(253))
