        context_item("Activity Synthesis", "To involve more students in the conversation, consider asking:\n\n- 'Who can restate 's reasoning in a different way?'\n- 'Did anyone use the same strategy but would explain it differently?'\n- 'Did anyone solve the problem in a different way?'\n- 'Does anyone want to add on to 's strategy?'\n- 'Do you agree or disagree? Why?'\n- 'What connections to previous problems do you see?'\n- Access for English Language Learners\n\n\nMLR8 Discussion Supports. Display sentence frames to support students when they explain their strategy. For example, 'First, I because . . . .' or 'I noticed so I . . . .' Some students may benefit from the opportunity to rehearse what they will say with a partner before they share with the whole class. Advances: Speaking, Representing", line(145)),
        context_item("Activity Synthesis", "The goal of this discussion is for students to connect that the exponent rules discovered for powers of 10 work with other bases. Discuss the following questions:\n\n- 'How does working with powers of 2 compare to working with powers of 10?' (The rules apply in the same way, but computing the value of some powers of 2 can take longer since we can't just look at the number of zeros.)\n- 'What happens when you use exponent rules when one of the exponents is 0?' (The rules apply in the same way. in the same way that .)\n- 'How does compare to ?' (Both values are positive and less than 1. When each is written as a fraction, has greater numbers in the denominator, so it is less than .)", line(275)),
        context_item("Activity Synthesis", "The goal of this discussion is for students to see that the exponent rules are valid for nonzero bases other than 10. For each list, invite students to share which expressions did not match the original expression and to share their reasoning strategies. If necessary for time, consider focusing just on questions 3 and 4 to assess how students have generalized in terms of variables and whether students have internalized the 0 exponent.\n\nAs students share their strategies for each list, discuss:\n\n- 'Do you agree or disagree? Why?'\n- 'Did anyone think about that expression in a different way?'\n- 'Did anyone use the same strategy but would explain it differently?'", line(379)),
        context_item("Lesson Synthesis", "The purpose of the discussion is to check whether students understand that the rules they have developed for doing arithmetic with powers of 10 are valid for any other nonzero rational bases as well. This is also a good opportunity to address possible misconceptions that students have, especially regarding non-positive exponents.\n\nConsider using these discussion questions to emphasize the important concepts in this lesson:\n\n- 'How do and compare to and ?' (When the base is 2 rather than 10, the repeated multiplication works the same way. is , and is .)\n- 'How do and compare to and ?' (The exponent rules work in exactly the same way. The base is just 5 instead of 10.)\n- 'How is different from ?' ( is whereas is .)\n- 'Do the other exponent rules work the same way that they did for base 10? Give some examples with different bases.' (Yes, the other exponent rules work the same way. With base 7 for example, we have that , and .)\n\nTell students that the exponent rules with other bases work exactly the same way as they did with a base of 10. They can always check this by expanding the factors of an expression that has exponents. Introduce and explain the visual display prepared earlier. This display should be kept visible to students throughout the remainder of the unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n$$( a ^ { n } ) ^ { m } = a ^ { n \\cdot m }$$\n\n$$\\frac { a ^ { n } } { a ^ { m } } = a ^ { n - m }$$\n\n$$a ^ { - n } = \\frac { 1 } { a ^ { n } }$$\n\n$$a ^ { 2 } \\cdot a ^ { 3 } = ( a \\cdot a ) \\cdot ( a \\cdot a \\cdot a ) = a ^ { 5 } \\\\ \\nearrow & \\quad \\nearrow \\\\ \\text {two factors} \\quad \\text {, three factors} \\quad \\text {, } & \\quad \\text {five factors} \\\\ \\text {that are a} & \\quad \\text {that are a}$$\n\n$$( a ^ { 2 } ) ^ { 3 } = ( \\underline { a \\cdot a } ) \\cdot ( \\underline { a \\cdot a } ) \\cdot ( \\underline { a \\cdot a } ) = a ^ { 6 }$$\n\n$$\\begin{array} { r l r } { \\text {three groups of} } & = } & { \\text {six factors} } \\\\ { \\text {two factors that are a} } & { \\text {that are a} } \\end{array}$$\n\n$$\\frac { a ^ { 5 } } { a ^ { 2 } } = \\frac { a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a } { a \\cdot a } = \\frac { a \\cdot a } { a \\cdot a } \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a = 1 \\cdot a ^ { 3 } = a ^ { 3 }$$\n\n$$\\begin{array} { r l r l } { f i v f a t s } & { \\div } & { t w o f a t s } & { = } & { t h e r e f a t s } \\\\ { t h a t a r e a } & { \\div } & { t h a t a r e a } & { = } & { t h a t a r e a } \\end{array}$$\n\n$$a ^ { - 3 } = \\frac { 1 } { a } \\cdot \\frac { 1 } { a } \\cdot \\frac { 1 } { a } = \\frac { 1 } { a ^ { 3 } }$$", line(389))
